A||||10||Homicidal||Car loses control/swirves badly after hitting bumps?!||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||08-31-2001||05:00 AM||||I got an 84' SE.  This problem has become increasingly worse, the car used to have great, tight handeling.  But now when I hit pretty strong bumps (and sometimes little ones) it makes the car swirve violently, and I find this extremely dangerous.  Plus, I notice if I shake the wheel slightly while driving, I can practically fishtail if I don't regain control.<P>I am exaggerating a bit on the control issue, but is it isn't too safe, and the car does shake way too much for hitting minor bumps.  <P>I'm a bit lost on what it could be, but my guess would be the bushings on the stabilizing bar or possibly shocks?  Any help is, as usual, appreciated greatly!    ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||08-31-2001||06:17 AM||||Could be your steering stabilizer.  It's the shock looking thing that mounts horizontally on your steering rack.  Jack up the front of your car.  Visually inspect everything to make sure nothing has come loose.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-31-2001||07:19 AM||||Id look for bad control arm bushings or ball joint. sounds like something is changing caster.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||08-31-2001||08:15 AM||||Due to the Fiero's suspension set-up it could be in the front or rear. I would check ball joints, tie rods, bushings, tires, bearings, and alignment. You state you hit bumps, you could have hit a big enough one to loosen or bend something.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||08-31-2001||09:46 AM||||How old are your shocks and struts?  It could be simple as replacing them.  NOT!  I'm sure it would help if there old, but then OLD ball joints, bushings, and/or steering components probably aren't doing the greatest job keeping the car under control either.  I would have changed my shocks and struts out years ago if I knew it would improve my handling characteristics they way it did.  <FONT COLOR="BLOODY">MUCH BETTER</FONT> through the bumps, corners and wiggles in the road.  Straight line racing, well any racing, is too hard on the drive train for me to do it to this car(primary car).  I only enjoy zipping around at a <FONT COLOR="#=0000ff"><FONT SIZE="3">"fun"</FONT></FONT> level of pleasure, not <I>abusive,</I> so my shocks are only Monroe road handlers.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000005||Butter||08-31-2001||11:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>Id look for bad control arm bushings or ball joint. sounds like something is changing caster.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When I bought the 84 I drive now it had a very similar problem.  It was the left rear ball joint.<BR>||||reg||1
